# QA Re-alignment Document — injectBadge

## Issues

- [x] **`src/index.ts:156` — `"badge"` missing from MCP `inputSchema.category` enum.**
  The Zod `InitInput` schema in `src/tools/init/index.ts:32` correctly includes
  `"badge"` in the `z.enum` array. But the raw `inputSchema` JSON object in
  `src/index.ts` — the declaration the MCP protocol surface exposes to clients —
  still lists only `["framework", "methodology", "commands", "agents", "skills",
  "mcp", "brain", "ide"]`. `"badge"` is absent. Clients that respect the schema
  declaration will not offer or accept `category: "badge"`. Agents that strictly
  follow the schema will never issue the category call that triggers the write path,
  making desired outcome 5 unreachable in practice.
  Traces to: desired outcome 5 — "When called with the category parameter, the tool
  writes the badge line directly into the README and returns a manifest entry."
  Misalignment: plan-gap (plan step 1 specified adding "badge" to `z.enum` but
  never mentioned updating the parallel `inputSchema` declaration in `src/index.ts`)

- [x] **`src/index.ts:141` — tool description wizard walkthrough omits `badge` from step 5 category sequence.**
  The tool description enumerates the wizard step order as: "methodology, commands,
  agents, skills, mcp, brain, ide". `badge` is not listed. An agent following the
  description literally will complete the wizard without presenting the badge
  category. This contradicts desired outcome 4, which requires the badge to appear
  in the default summary and be presentable without a follow-up call.
  Traces to: desired outcome 4 — "The default init call includes this category in
  its metadata summary … The calling agent can present this to the user without
  issuing a category-specific follow-up."
  Misalignment: implementation-drift (the implementation wires the badge step into
  the steps array, but the prose instructions that drive agent behavior were not
  updated to mention it)

## Retro

**Issue 1 (inputSchema enum missing "badge")** would have been caught if plan step 1
had distinguished between the two places `"badge"` needs to be registered: the Zod
schema (`InitInput`) and the MCP protocol declaration (`inputSchema` in `src/index.ts`).
These are parallel structures that must be kept in sync by hand — neither the TypeScript
compiler nor the Zod schema enforces their agreement. The plan named only "the `z.enum`
array in the `category` field of `InitInput`", which was done. The raw schema was not
on the plan's radar. Fix: whenever a new category value is added, the plan must call
out both the Zod schema and the `inputSchema` JSON object as distinct line items, not
combine them into a single step.

**Issue 2 (wizard description missing "badge")** would have been caught by reading the
full tool description when verifying the wiring step. The description is long prose and
contains the authoritative list of wizard categories an agent will walk — any category
not mentioned there is effectively invisible to agents that follow it. When the
implementor added the badge step to the `steps` array (plan step 3), they did not
revisit the tool description to append the new category. A wiring checklist that
explicitly includes "update tool description category sequence" would prevent this.
